  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\n\nA trend of warming temperatures is still forecasted into next \nweek as a result of the surface high currently affecting the \nregion begins to traverse eastward. This will cause the western \nhalf of the high pressure to shift our winds into a southerly \nmotion, bringing in the aforementioned warmer temperatures. \nTemperatures are expected to be in the lower 60s across the CWA \nby Wednesday. \n\nTuesday and Wednesday are expected to see more breezy conditions as \na tightening PGF sets in over the Great Plains region in response to \na low pressure system expected to eject across the Central CONUS. \nEPS guidance shows wind gusts in excess of 30-40 mph. This wind will \nalso help to advect moisture ahead of an associated cold front. This \nfront will impact the CWA Wednesday into Thursday, giving us the \nnext chance for rain (20-30%). Tracking and timing of this rain \nstill remains uncertain, hence the 20-30% chance range. \n\nModels are hinting at a potential disturbance moving through \nsometime late next Friday into the weekend. Details remain uncertain \non any potential impacts like precipitation type and amounts. Trends \nwill continue to be monitored through the next several days.",
